  "whyItMatters": "A rigid end offset says where the flexible length of a member starts: the part inside the joint (half the depth of the column or beam it frames into) does not bend. It shortens the flexible span and moves the point where the member end forces act on the joint, which adds the moment V x e to the joint. With the sign reversed the flexible end lies on the wrong side of the node: the flexible length grows by twice the offset instead of shrinking, and the eccentricity moment changes sign.",
